#{[17592186516825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516825 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516825 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516825 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $△ABC∼△LMN,$ $BC=4\\,ft$ and $CA=2\\,ft,$\nwhich of the following could be the values of $MN$ and $NL?$\n\nI. $MN=2\\,ft,\\,NL=1\\,ft$\n\nII. $MN=12\\,ft,\\,NL=6\\,ft$\n\nIII. $MN=10\\,ft,\\,NL=8\\,ft$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d0a74cc-718a-5740-8268-b7272012d842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516825 #edb/ref 1074 "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- Let's check option I:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{BC}{MN} & =\\frac{4}{2}=2, \\\\ \\frac{CA}{NL} & =\\frac{2}{1}=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I does give possible values of $MN$ and $NL.$\n\n- For option II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{BC}{MN} & =\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\frac{CA}{NL} & =\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option II does give possible values of $MN$ and $NL.$\n\n- For option III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{BC}{MN} & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\frac{CA}{NL} & =\\frac{2}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III does not give possible values of $MN$ and $NL.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121176" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516860 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/14/145db132983ccf795c8fc466cd6e1ce77b7a33a3119fb281c3592ad5234cb08a.png)\n\nThe two quadrilaterals shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7e53512-d30f-5e5f-8560-8aa96c633833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516860 #edb/ref 1074 "Since, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠P,\\,∠B≅∠S,\\,∠C≅∠R,\\,∠D≅∠Q,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $P,$\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $S,$\n\n- $C$ corresponds to $R,$\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $Q.$\n\nTherefore, $ABCD∼PSRQ.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516863 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53576" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516863 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e9/e9fa701b3efb9251e5591c40fe8c5011a73cd2b7794f6ce356860b2eeb1d8001.png)\n\nThe two quadrilaterals shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f26dd26e-832a-57e0-87b3-fbf105b066fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516863 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is ${360}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {68}^{∘}+m∠B+{85}^{∘}+{111}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+{264}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={96}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠M+m∠N+m∠K+m∠L & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {68}^{∘}+{96}^{∘}+m∠K+{111}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠K+{275}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠K & ={85}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠M,\\,∠B≅∠N,\\,∠C≅∠K,\\,∠D≅∠L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $M,$\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $N,$\n\n- $C$ corresponds to $K,$\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $L.$\n\nTherefore, $ABCD∼MNKL.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
